rding the equation as a quadratic in \\\\(x\\\\),\\n\\\\[\\nx^2+(1-4m_k)x+(m_k^2+m_k)=0,\\n\\\\]\\none root is \\\\(n_k\\\\). By Vieta's formula, the other root is\\n\\\\[\\n4m_k-1-n_k=m_{k+1}.\\n\\\\]\\nHence \\\\(F(m_k,m_{k+1})=0\\\\), and by symmetry\\n\\\\[\\nF(m_{k+1},n_{k+1})=F(m_{k+1},m_k)=0.\\n\\\\]\\nThus every recursively constructed pair satisfies\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{m_k+1}{n_k}+\\\\frac{n_k+1}{m_k}=4.\\n\\\\]\\n\\nIt remains to check positivity and distinctness. Initially \\\\(m_0>n_0\\\\ge1\\\\). If \\\\(m_k>n_k\\\\ge1\\\\), then\\n\\\\[\\nm_{k+1}-n_{k+1}=3m_k-n_k-1>0,\\n\\\\]\\nand \\\\(n_{k+1}=m_k\\\\ge1\\\\). Therefore \\\\(m_k>n_k\\\\ge1\\\\) for all \\\\(k\\\\). Moreover,\\n\\\\[\\nn_{k+1}=m_k>n_k,\\n\\\\]\\nso the sequence \\\\((n_k)\\\\) is strictly increasing and the pairs are all distinct. Consequently there are infinitely many pairs of positive integers with the required expression a positive integer.\"}, {\"candidate_id\": \"choice-3\", \"text\": \"Define integer sequences by\\n\\\\[\\n(t_0,y_0)=(1,1),\\\\qquad t_{k+1}=2t_k+3y_k,\\\\quad y_{k+1}=t_k+2y_k.\\n\\\\]\\nSince\\n\\\\[\\nt_{k+1}^2-3y_{k+1}^2=t_k^2-3y_k^2,\\n\\\\]\\ninduction gives\\n\\\\[\\nt_k^2-3y_k^2=-2 \\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\nfor every \\\\(k\\\\ge0\\\\). Moreover, \\\\(t_k,y_k\\\\) are positive odd integers: this holds initially, and the recurrence preserves positivity and oddness. Also \\\\(y_{k+1}=t_k+2y_k>y_k\\\\), so the \\\\(y_k\\\\) are all distinct.\\n\\nSet\\n\\\\[\\nn_k=\\\\frac{y_k+1}{2},\\\\qquad m_k=\\\\frac{4n_k-1+t_k}{2}.\\n\\\\]\\nBoth are integers because \\\\(y_k\\\\) and \\\\(t_k\\\\) are odd, and they are positive. From \\\\(y_k=2n_k-1\\\\) and (1),\\n\\\\[\\nt_k^2=3(2n_k-1)^2-2=12n_k^2-12n_k+1.\\n\\\\]\\nThis is the discriminant of\\n\\\\[\\nm^2+(1-4n_k)m+n_k^2+n_k=0.\\n\\\\]\\nThus the chosen value \\\\(m_k=(4n_k-1+t_k)/2\\\\) satisfies\\n\\\\[\\nm_k^2+n_k^2+m_k+n_k-4m_kn_k=0.\\n\\\\]\\nDividing by the positive integer \\\\(m_kn_k\\\\) yields\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\frac{m_k+1}{n_k}+\\\\frac{n_k+1}{m_k}=4,\\n\\\\]\\nwhich is a positive integer. Since the strictly increasing values \\\\(y_k\\\\) give strictly increasing values \\\\(n_k=(y_k+1)/2\\\\), the pairs \\\\((m_k,n_k)\\\\) are all distinct. Hence there are infinitely many pairs of positive integers with the required property.\"}]}",
